From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 05:32:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: power: apple,pmgr-s2r: describe
 asahi,s2r-reset-vector

The boot CPU comes back from suspend-to-RAM at the address in its RVBAR.
The mailbox alone does not tell the kernel what is there. On the J604 the
RVBAR is locked by the first m1n1 stage, before the chainloaded one that
publishes the mailbox runs, so the two can disagree.

Firmware that verified every core's RVBAR against its own warm boot
vector now names that vector in /chosen/asahi,s2r-reset-vector, next to
the mailbox. Describe the property and what the kernel does with it.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 .../devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-s2r.yaml          | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-s2r.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-s2r.yaml
index cd4cfc3249..d00b0fb4dc 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-s2r.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/power/apple,pmgr-s2r.yaml
@@ -32,6 +32,13 @@ description: |
   firmware when it took the warm boot path). The region is reserved
   memory. Without the property, suspend-to-RAM is not offered.
 
+  The boot CPU restarts at the address in its RVBAR. Firmware that publishes
+  the mailbox only after it found every core's RVBAR locked to its own warm
+  boot vector names that vector in the 64-bit
+  /chosen/asahi,s2r-reset-vector property. The kernel requires it next to
+  the mailbox, and offers or enters suspend-to-RAM only while the live RVBAR
+  (read through each CPU node's apple,cpu-impl-reg) is locked to it.
+
 properties:
   $nodename:
     pattern: "^power-management@[0-9a-f]+$"
